From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tim 'mithro' Ansell <me@mith.ro>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 02:23:06 +0930
Subject: [PATCH] Fix GENET RX: fill the status block rx_csum with a real
 packet checksum

The Linux bcmgenet driver (all kernels with RSB support, including the
6.12 and 6.18 Raspberry Pi OS kernels) consumes the rx_csum field of
the 64-byte Receive Status Block as a CHECKSUM_COMPLETE value:

    rx_csum = (__force __be16)(status->rx_csum & 0xffff);
    if (rx_csum) {
        skb->csum = (__force __wsum)ntohs(rx_csum);
        skb->ip_summed = CHECKSUM_COMPLETE;
    }

CHECKSUM_COMPLETE means "the folded 16-bit one's-complement sum of the
frame after the Ethernet header, exactly as csum_partial() would
compute it".  On real hardware the RXCHK block provides this when
RBUF_RXCHK_EN is set (bcmgenet_init_umac() always sets it).

The emulation instead stored the received frame's IPv4 *header
checksum field* (ip_sum) -- an unrelated value that is never the
packet sum.  Every received IPv4 packet therefore advertised a bogus
hardware checksum, with two consequences on every Linux guest:

 * the kernel logs "eth0: hw csum failure" (netdev_rx_csum_fault) with
   a full skb dump the first time a checksum is consumed, surfacing
   through __skb_gro_checksum_complete/udp4_gro_receive or
   __skb_checksum_complete/tcp_v4_rcv;

 * every received TCP/UDP segment fails the hardware checksum
   validation and only survives by taking a full software
   csum_partial() pass over the payload
   (__skb_gro_checksum_complete()/__skb_checksum_complete() fallback),
   so the "hardware" offload adds work instead of removing it on
   every single packet.  This is the RX-path overhead observed
   alongside the SSH throughput collapse in
   fpgas-online/fpgas.online-infra#37 CI (issue #12).

Replace it with the checksum the driver expects: the folded (not
complemented) one's-complement sum over the frame payload starting
after the 14-byte Ethernet header, gated on RBUF_RXCHK_EN.  The value
is stored in the guest-endian (LE) status block such that the driver's
ntohs() recovers exactly what csum_partial() computes over the same
bytes, so the kernel's checksum validation now succeeds without the
software fallback and GRO coalescing works.

The eth_get_protocols() call and its outputs were only used to fetch
the IPv4 header checksum field and are removed.

A/B tested with a slirp guest fetching 20 x 1 MB chunks over one TCP
connection (repro for issue #12): with the unpatched device model BOTH
the 6.18.39+rpt-rpi-v8 and 6.12.96+rpt-rpi-v8 kernels log the
"hw csum failure" splat on first traffic; with this fix neither does,
and all 20 chunks arrive md5-intact on both kernels (no regression).

Fixes: https://github.com/fpgas-online/rpi-qemu/issues/12
Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 hw/net/bcm2838_genet.c | 36 +++++++++++++++++++++---------------
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 15 deletions(-)

diff --git a/hw/net/bcm2838_genet.c b/hw/net/bcm2838_genet.c
index 476f692..13f4b00 100644
--- a/hw/net/bcm2838_genet.c
+++ b/hw/net/bcm2838_genet.c
@@ -856,12 +856,6 @@ static ssize_t bcm2838_genet_rdma(BCM2838GenetState *s, uint32_t ring_idx,
         MemTxResult mem_tx_result = MEMTX_OK;
         uint8_t *frame_buf = dma_buf + sizeof(BCM2838GenetXmitStatus) + 2;
         BCM2838GenetXmitStatus *xmit_status = (BCM2838GenetXmitStatus *)dma_buf;
-        struct iovec iov;
-        bool isip4, isip6;
-        size_t l3hdr_off, l4hdr_off, l5hdr_off;
-        eth_ip6_hdr_info ip6hdr_info;
-        eth_ip4_hdr_info ip4hdr_info;
-        eth_l4_hdr_info  l4hdr_info;
 
         bool crc_fwd = FIELD_EX32(s->regs.umac.cmd, GENET_UMAC_CMD, CRC_FWD);
         bool rbuf_64b_en = !!(s->regs.rbuf.ctrl & 1); /* RBUF_64B_EN */
@@ -883,12 +877,6 @@ static ssize_t bcm2838_genet_rdma(BCM2838GenetState *s, uint32_t ring_idx,
         }
 
         memcpy(frame_buf, buf + len, l);
-        iov.iov_base = frame_buf;
-        iov.iov_len = l;
-        eth_get_protocols(&iov, 1, 0,
-                          &isip4, &isip6,
-                          &l3hdr_off, &l4hdr_off, &l5hdr_off,
-                          &ip6hdr_info, &ip4hdr_info, &l4hdr_info);
 
         len += l;
 
@@ -915,10 +903,28 @@ static ssize_t bcm2838_genet_rdma(BCM2838GenetState *s, uint32_t ring_idx,
                                          !!is_packet_multicast(frame_buf, l));
 
         if (rbuf_64b_en) {
-            xmit_status->rx_csum = 0;
-            if (isip4) {
-                xmit_status->rx_csum = ip4hdr_info.ip4_hdr.ip_sum;
+            /*
+             * The Linux driver consumes rx_csum from the Receive Status
+             * Block as a CHECKSUM_COMPLETE value: the folded 16-bit
+             * one's-complement sum of the frame after the Ethernet
+             * header, computed by the RXCHK block when RBUF_RXCHK_EN
+             * is set.  It must match what the kernel's csum_partial()
+             * computes over the same bytes, or the kernel flags every
+             * packet as a hardware checksum fault ("hw csum failure")
+             * and falls back to software validation.
+             */
+            uint32_t rx_csum = 0;
+            bool rxchk_en = !!(s->regs.rbuf.chk_ctrl & 1); /* RBUF_RXCHK_EN */
+
+            if (rxchk_en && l > ETH_HLEN) {
+                uint32_t sum = net_checksum_add(l - ETH_HLEN,
+                                                frame_buf + ETH_HLEN);
+                /* Fold to 16 bits; do not complement. */
+                sum = (sum & 0xffff) + (sum >> 16);
+                sum = (sum & 0xffff) + (sum >> 16);
+                rx_csum = sum;
             }
+            xmit_status->rx_csum = rx_csum;
             xmit_status->length_status = desc->length_status;
         }
 
-- 
2.55.0

